{
  "title": "Lago \u2014 fintech / billing infrastructure (9,708\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Lago\nDomain: fintech / billing infrastructure | Scale: large | Stars: 9,708\nProblem: Lago is an open-source metering and usage-based billing platform that enables product-led companies to track consumption, manage subscriptions, and automate revenue collection. It solves the complexity of implementing flexible pricing models (usage-based, subscription, hybrid) with integrated payment orchestration and revenue analytics.\nTarget users: Product-led SaaS companies, Platform and marketplace operators, Fintech companies, Enterprise billing teams, Companies with usage-based or hybrid pricing models\nCore features: Usage Metering - Real-time event ingestion and aggregation; Billing & Invoicing - Automated invoice generation; Payment Orchestration - Multi-gateway support with dunning and retries; Revenue Analytics - MRR visibility and usage trends; Entitlements - Feature access management tied to billing plans; Lago Embedded - White-label billing for platforms; AI-powered billing intelligence and MCP server; Enterprise integrations - NetSuite, Xero, Salesforce, Avalara\nIntegrations: Stripe, Adyen, GoCardless, NetSuite, Xero, Salesforce, HubSpot, Avalara, AWS Marketplace, Azure Marketplace, GCP Marketplace\nTech stack: React + Go, Ruby + ClickHouse (for analytics/events) + Docker, Docker Compose\nKey libraries: ClickHouse (event ingestion and aggregation), OpenAPI (API specification), RSA encryption (for key management)\nArchitecture: microservices\nDesign lessons:\n- Event-driven architecture with ClickHouse for high-volume consumption event ingestion and aggregation, enabling real-time metering at scale\n- API-first design philosophy with comprehensive REST API coverage and multi-language SDK support (Node.js, Python, Ruby, Go) for developer accessibility\n- Self-hosted and SaaS deployment flexibility via Docker Compose with careful consideration for configuration (S3/GCS storage, SMTP, SSL, dedicated workers) to support both small and enterprise deployments\n- Polyglot backend approach using both Go and Ruby, likely optimizing for Go's performance in high-throughput event processing and Ruby for business logic and admin operations",
